And he got his family.

1756
01:25:42.290 --> 01:25:44.650
So he doesn't need this other thing anymore.

1757
01:25:44.930 --> 01:25:45.210
Yes.

1758
01:25:45.210 --> 01:25:48.150
And this is a departure from the British office.

1759
01:25:48.710 --> 01:25:56.490
In the Christmas special for the British office, their documentary has aired and David Brent has become kind of a celebrity.

1760
01:25:56.830 --> 01:26:02.470
And so in that episode, he is just sort of reveling in his new fame.

1761
01:26:02.650 --> 01:26:05.530
And I think there was also a desire to not repeat.

1762
01:26:06.010 --> 01:26:06.550
Yeah.

1763
01:26:06.710 --> 01:26:10.790
And I also think that spot was already filled in our story with Andy.

1764
01:26:11.150 --> 01:26:11.750
Exactly.

1765
01:26:12.750 --> 01:26:13.370
All right.

1766
01:26:13.470 --> 01:26:15.090
Here's who else was not on the panel.

1767
01:26:16.130 --> 01:26:17.510
Kelly and Ryan.

1768
01:26:18.590 --> 01:26:20.190
They do show up at the wedding.

1769
01:26:20.490 --> 01:26:20.830
They do.

1770
01:26:21.230 --> 01:26:27.470
Now, I 100% think that Kelly would want to be the center of attention on a panel.

1771
01:26:28.310 --> 01:26:30.870
Unfortunately, Mindy Kaling was not available this day.

1772
01:26:31.010 --> 01:26:32.870
And neither was BJ Novak.

1773
01:26:33.010 --> 01:26:35.010
So that's why they weren't on the panel.

1774
01:26:35.390 --> 01:26:38.450
We talked about Nellie, who shows up at the wedding.

1775
01:26:38.650 --> 01:26:40.070
We don't know why she wasn't there.

1776
01:26:40.270 --> 01:26:41.070
Something happened.

1777
01:26:41.070 --> 01:26:42.690
We have not gotten to the bottom of it.

1778
01:26:42.770 --> 01:26:44.410
I feel like it was a scheduling thing as well.

1779
01:26:44.690 --> 01:26:55.410
But here are some other people who I thought, if I'm tracking this as like the documentary bringing people back for fans to talk to, here's who I thought should have been there.

1780
01:26:56.030 --> 01:26:56.550
Jan.

1781
01:26:56.890 --> 01:26:57.950
For sure.

1782
01:26:58.530 --> 01:26:59.030
Roy.

1783
01:26:59.550 --> 01:27:00.030
Oh, yeah.

1784
01:27:00.490 --> 01:27:01.350
Todd Packer.

1785
01:27:01.490 --> 01:27:02.650
He would have loved it.

1786
01:27:03.010 --> 01:27:03.390
Gabe.

1787
01:27:05.330 --> 01:27:06.370
Robert California.

1788
01:27:06.630 --> 01:27:07.670
Oh, that would have been delicious.

1789
01:27:08.170 --> 01:27:08.590
Karen.

1790
01:27:08.590 --> 01:27:09.410
Oh, yeah.

1791
01:27:10.190 --> 01:27:12.130
And there's one more person who's not there.

1792
01:27:12.370 --> 01:27:12.930
Oh, yeah.

1793
01:27:13.110 --> 01:27:14.150
It's you, Angela.

1794
01:27:14.310 --> 01:27:14.770
I know.

1795
01:27:15.150 --> 01:27:17.350
Do you want to share with everyone why you weren't there?

1796
01:27:17.550 --> 01:27:21.250
I know Dwight says it's because you guys shouldn't see each other before the wedding.

1797
01:27:21.390 --> 01:27:23.530
But there's a real reason why you weren't there.

1798
01:27:23.710 --> 01:27:24.490
There is.

1799
01:27:24.490 --> 01:27:26.090
So here's the deal.

1800
01:27:26.730 --> 01:27:32.170
As we started filming the finale, I was up for a really big role on a new sitcom.

1801
01:27:32.510 --> 01:27:35.330
It was a big deal for me because it was a lead.

1802
01:27:35.590 --> 01:27:40.350
The show was called The Gabriels, and it would have been me and Rob Riggle playing husband and wife.

1803
01:27:41.190 --> 01:27:42.590
Tim Meadows was in it.

1804
01:27:42.590 --> 01:27:43.970
It was really great.

1805
01:27:44.090 --> 01:27:45.130
The cast was phenomenal.

1806
01:27:45.990 --> 01:27:51.610
And, you know, it's just one of those things that happens in life when everything you want to do all happens at the same time.

1807
01:27:51.790 --> 01:27:52.110
Yes.

1808
01:27:52.490 --> 01:27:56.210
So they were both going to overlap the finale in this new pilot.

1809
01:27:56.210 --> 01:27:59.910
I didn't know what to do, and I went to Greg, and I said, Greg, what should I do?

1810
01:28:00.310 --> 01:28:07.090
And, you know, Jenna, he was so kind, and he said, listen, I want this opportunity to happen for you, but I honestly don't know how to make it work.

1811
01:28:07.090 --> 01:28:13.210
He said if Steve Burgess could work his scheduling magic and figure it out, I had his blessing.

1812
01:28:13.890 --> 01:28:21.750
And creatively, he said he could take me out of this panel, that he could make it make sense for him, for the show, Why Angela Wouldn't Be There.

1813
01:28:21.870 --> 01:28:22.110
Sure.

1814
01:28:22.250 --> 01:28:23.650
It's the morning of her wedding.

1815
01:28:23.650 --> 01:28:27.250
He could make that creatively make sense, and that would give me an extra day.

1816
01:28:27.690 --> 01:28:33.590
But then Steve Burgess rolled up his sleeve, and, you know, he's always been such a champion for us.

1817
01:28:33.590 --> 01:28:41.350
And he knew, Jenna, that me playing the lead in something would mean I would get considered for bigger roles after The Office wrapped.

1818
01:28:42.130 --> 01:28:44.570
And I just love this man so much.

1819
01:28:44.670 --> 01:28:46.890
He got on the phone with the producers at Fox.

1820
01:28:47.090 --> 01:28:48.690
They worked it all out.

1821
01:28:49.350 --> 01:28:52.130
It included some crazy scheduling.

1822
01:28:52.130 --> 01:28:55.330
I had to film part of the Fox pilot on a Saturday.

1823
01:28:55.610 --> 01:28:57.930
On one day, I worked on both shows.

1824
01:28:58.430 --> 01:29:03.030
Steve Burgess sent a van to pick me up on The Fox lot with the office hair and makeup team.

1825
01:29:03.030 --> 01:29:08.050
And they transformed me into Angela Martin driving down the freeway.

1826
01:29:08.390 --> 01:29:08.910
Amazing.

1827
01:29:09.270 --> 01:29:13.050
I asked Steve what he remembered about all of this, and this is what he had to say.

1828
01:29:13.250 --> 01:29:19.310
One of my favorite things was talking with Kim Ferry about how she had to redo your hair on the drive to the set.

1829
01:29:19.590 --> 01:29:21.690
Kim and I still laugh and talk about that.

1830
01:29:21.930 --> 01:29:23.510
But that was just part of it.

1831
01:29:23.650 --> 01:29:25.850
I spoke with the pilot people all the time.

1832
01:29:25.850 --> 01:29:30.190
We worked out a schedule that we thought would work but unfortunately might actually kill you.

1833
01:29:30.390 --> 01:29:31.690
But you survived.

1834
01:29:31.690 --> 01:29:34.470
Both schedules had to change a lot during the shooting.

1835
01:29:34.650 --> 01:29:35.370
We ran long.

1836
01:29:35.530 --> 01:29:36.770
They had to reschedule stuff.

1837
01:29:36.910 --> 01:29:38.150
But it all worked out.

1838
01:29:38.310 --> 01:29:42.950
You were amazing shooting two shows, both demanding, and you came through looking great.

1839
01:29:43.270 --> 01:29:47.230
Well, I remember that day that you arrived to set and you had gotten ready in the van.

1840
01:29:48.170 --> 01:29:49.930
It was so crazy.

1841
01:29:51.090 --> 01:29:58.090
You know, when I watch these scenes, I do get a little emotional because, oh, obviously for so many reasons,

1842
01:29:58.270 --> 01:30:05.250
but also because Greg and Steve did that for me because they knew it would help my career after the office.

1843
01:30:05.450 --> 01:30:06.670
And that was such a gift.

1844
01:30:06.670 --> 01:30:11.610
And I did find something really special to me in my digital clutter about it.

1845
01:30:12.490 --> 01:30:18.870
I sent two people an email about my new look on the Gabriels, my fancy hair.

1846
01:30:19.130 --> 01:30:21.850
I emailed you and my dad.

1847
01:30:22.230 --> 01:30:27.010
I sent you both a picture and I said, this is my new look for the Gabriels.

1848
01:30:27.210 --> 01:30:30.550
You wrote back, lady, you look amazing!

1849
01:30:30.910 --> 01:30:32.130
Two exclamation points.

1850
01:30:32.130 --> 01:30:38.070
And my dad wrote, Ange, I like it, LOL, which he thought meant lots of love.

1851
01:30:38.190 --> 01:30:38.450
Yes.

1852
01:30:38.670 --> 01:30:39.290
Bill Kinsey.

1853
01:30:40.150 --> 01:30:40.490
Hmm.

1854
01:30:41.550 --> 01:30:42.730
I love that.

1855
01:30:43.850 --> 01:30:47.530
Well, it was a little bittersweet for me, Angela, that you weren't there.

1856
01:30:47.770 --> 01:30:52.170
This was my second favorite scene that we shot for the finale.

1857
01:30:52.450 --> 01:30:54.090
You said it was really moving.

1858
01:30:54.690 --> 01:30:55.930
It really was.

1859
01:30:55.930 --> 01:31:04.110
It was special because Greg filled this scene with so much of our crew and our office family.

1860
01:31:04.790 --> 01:31:07.990
You know, Greg created a type of yearbook for us.

1861
01:31:08.090 --> 01:31:13.870
And when I was rewatching the finale, and in particular this scene, I saw all those familiar faces.

1862
01:31:13.870 --> 01:31:19.930
And I was so grateful to him that we have these people on camera.

1863
01:31:20.790 --> 01:31:24.530
So he made our writers and other crew members.

1864
01:31:25.390 --> 01:31:28.090
He gave them roles asking the questions.

1865
01:31:28.510 --> 01:31:35.090
And he said it really worked because if someone was a little stiff or maybe not the best actor,

1866
01:31:35.090 --> 01:31:39.030
it's like, well, that's what a real person in the audience would be like.

1867
01:31:39.210 --> 01:31:43.630
Well, you know, I think we should do a breakdown as well of the special guests that are in this scene.

1868
01:31:43.750 --> 01:31:44.890
Yes, let's talk about it.

1869
01:31:45.150 --> 01:31:47.730
The moderator is played by Suzanne Daniels.

1870
01:31:47.950 --> 01:31:48.890
Greg's wife.

1871
01:31:49.210 --> 01:31:51.170
I thought she did a fantastic job.

1872
01:31:51.170 --> 01:31:54.570
The first question was asked by Brent Forrester.

1873
01:31:55.530 --> 01:31:58.390
Amelie Gillette asked about Jim giving up athlete.

1874
01:31:59.050 --> 01:32:03.550
Writer Allison Silverman said Jim could do anything, and I mean anything.

1875
01:32:03.830 --> 01:32:04.630
She was so funny.

1876
01:32:04.710 --> 01:32:05.550
So funny.

1877
01:32:05.850 --> 01:32:09.910
But also, I really loved as Pam saying, I'm sorry, was there a question there?

1878
01:32:10.370 --> 01:32:14.110
Also, writer Dan Sterling asked what was in the teapot letter.

1879
01:32:14.110 --> 01:32:17.570
Steve Healy asked if our lives felt pointless.

1880
01:32:18.590 --> 01:32:27.490
And Jenny Tan from Office Tally, this fan site that supported us from the very beginning, asked the question about Jim and Pam.

1881
01:32:28.010 --> 01:32:30.030
You know, I think that one was my favorite.

1882
01:32:30.550 --> 01:32:35.130
Jenny had originally asked Greg if she could just come and watch us film the finale.

1883
01:32:35.130 --> 01:32:39.690
And she wanted to do, like, a really nice farewell to the show.

1884
01:32:39.790 --> 01:32:42.810
And we'd given her kind of inside access before.

1885
01:32:43.110 --> 01:32:51.110
Guys, she truly was just a fan who started a fan site, but it was so good that she became a member of our office family.

1886
01:32:51.290 --> 01:33:01.830
Yes, and, you know, her site, Office Tally, it really was the place to go for all the inside tidbits that was happening on the show.

1887
01:33:01.930 --> 01:33:02.350
Oh, yeah.

1888
01:33:02.510 --> 01:33:04.030
She did such a good job.

1889
01:33:04.030 --> 01:33:09.230
Writers and directors would do, like, exclusive interviews with her after episodes aired.

1890
01:33:09.370 --> 01:33:10.670
It's all still there.

1891
01:33:11.030 --> 01:33:12.030
OfficeTally.com.

1892
01:33:12.170 --> 01:33:13.990
I refer to it sometimes for the pod.

1893
01:33:14.230 --> 01:33:14.390
Yeah.

1894
01:33:14.910 --> 01:33:17.650
Well, Greg said, yes, come out while we're filming the finale.

1895
01:33:17.850 --> 01:33:21.470
And she said, Greg, could I just be in the background of something?

1896
01:33:21.650 --> 01:33:23.530
I would just love to be in the background.

1897
01:33:23.790 --> 01:33:24.670
In the world somehow.

1898
01:33:25.310 --> 01:33:29.010
He said, no, you can't be in the background.

1899
01:33:29.150 --> 01:33:30.970
I'm going to give you seven lines.

1900
01:33:31.330 --> 01:33:32.810
She about pooped her pants.

1901
01:33:32.810 --> 01:33:34.110
She said she was so nervous.

1902
01:33:34.110 --> 01:33:35.930
She did so great.

1903
01:33:35.930 --> 01:33:36.970
She did fantastic.

1904
01:33:37.470 --> 01:33:41.690
But there is one other person who asked us a question, but it got cut.

1905
01:33:41.870 --> 01:33:44.710
And it is our very own Steve Burgess.

1906
01:33:44.890 --> 01:33:45.270
Yes.

1907
01:33:45.610 --> 01:33:46.750
And you know what, Steve?

1908
01:33:47.090 --> 01:33:50.490
It might have been cut, but it's in deleted scenes on the DVD.

1909
01:33:50.930 --> 01:33:54.250
And I think we need to hear Steve's voice on our podcaSt.

1910
01:33:54.610 --> 01:33:55.210
Let's hear it.

1911
01:33:55.870 --> 01:33:58.130
How did being in the documentary change your behavior?

1912
01:33:59.090 --> 01:34:00.110
There you have it.

1913
01:34:00.710 --> 01:34:03.350
That was our sweet Steve Burgess.

1914
01:34:03.650 --> 01:34:04.730
Steve, you're on the pod.

1915
01:34:04.870 --> 01:34:07.030
Although we want to have Steve on for a sit down.

1916
01:34:07.210 --> 01:34:07.890
For sure.

1917
01:34:08.190 --> 01:34:09.850
There's so many people we still want to talk to.

1918
01:34:10.250 --> 01:34:10.830
All right.

1919
01:34:11.690 --> 01:34:13.310
This panel is not over.

1920
01:34:13.310 --> 01:34:13.950
No.

1921
01:34:14.170 --> 01:34:18.150
It is going to end in a very emotional way that gets me every time.

1922
01:34:18.550 --> 01:34:24.850
The final person to ask a question is a woman who stands up and asks Erin if she hates her mom.

1923
01:34:25.290 --> 01:34:27.270
She says, you must really hate her.

1924
01:34:27.450 --> 01:34:29.230
You must be so angry at her.

1925
01:34:29.450 --> 01:34:32.930
And Erin says, well, I don't know if I would hate her.

1926
01:34:33.010 --> 01:34:35.350
I mean, she has this sweet thing she says.

1927
01:34:35.350 --> 01:34:41.750
Yeah, she says maybe sometimes, but not like hate, hate, more just like, mom, I hate you.

1928
01:34:41.870 --> 01:34:44.130
And then she would say, go to your room, young lady.

1929
01:34:44.270 --> 01:34:49.090
And then I'd stomp my foot and I'd run upstairs and I'd have a room, which is really cool.

1930
01:34:49.290 --> 01:34:50.490
And then we'd just have dinner.

1931
01:34:50.870 --> 01:34:51.730
But I don't know.

1932
01:34:52.130 --> 01:34:53.130
I'd have to meet her.

1933
01:34:53.790 --> 01:34:54.190
Thanks.

1934
01:34:54.930 --> 01:34:56.870
And then there's this long pause.

1935
01:34:59.190 --> 01:34:59.990
Joan Cusack.

1936
01:35:00.090 --> 01:35:00.730
I know.

1937
01:35:00.730 --> 01:35:08.630
As Erin is saying her lines, the emotion that comes over Joan's face is so moving.

1938
01:35:08.950 --> 01:35:09.450
Yeah.

1939
01:35:09.790 --> 01:35:11.630
I was tearing up watching it.

1940
01:35:11.710 --> 01:35:13.510
I can't imagine what it was like being there.

1941
01:35:13.670 --> 01:35:17.730
I saw Phyllis in the background sobbing in real life.

1942
01:35:17.910 --> 01:35:19.070
Those were real tears.

1943
01:35:19.770 --> 01:35:24.430
Angela, Joan's performance, it took our breath away.

1944
01:35:24.810 --> 01:35:25.030
Yeah.

1945
01:35:25.210 --> 01:35:28.310
I mean, we were just all on this panel.

1946
01:35:28.310 --> 01:35:32.150
And from the second she stepped up to the microphone, I got a lump in my throat.

1947
01:35:32.330 --> 01:35:33.650
I was like, oh, my gosh.

1948
01:35:33.870 --> 01:35:34.130
Yeah.

1949
01:35:34.350 --> 01:35:35.530
I knew what was going to happen.

1950
01:35:35.870 --> 01:35:37.210
It was truly emotional.

1951
01:35:37.890 --> 01:35:39.690
Erin realizes this is her mom.

1952
01:35:39.690 --> 01:35:43.610
And then, oh, my gosh, you thought you couldn't cry anymore.

1953
01:35:43.950 --> 01:35:44.710
Ed Begley Jr.

1954
01:35:45.010 --> 01:35:47.510
stands up and says, same question, but about your dad.

1955
01:35:48.190 --> 01:35:49.730
Erin has found her parents.

1956
01:35:50.050 --> 01:35:50.610
Her parents.

1957
01:35:52.110 --> 01:35:53.910
We did get a fan mail flurry.

1958
01:35:54.510 --> 01:35:57.110
Are Pete and Erin still together in the finale?

1959
01:35:57.110 --> 01:35:57.750
Hmm.

1960
01:35:58.170 --> 01:36:01.490
They sat apart during the panel, and they don't appear together at the wedding.

1961
01:36:01.730 --> 01:36:02.390
Please discuss.

1962
01:36:02.970 --> 01:36:07.650
Well, we asked Jake Lacey this question when we interviewed him, and he said he doesn't

1963
01:36:07.650 --> 01:36:08.290
think they are.

1964
01:36:08.910 --> 01:36:09.170
Yeah.

1965
01:36:09.590 --> 01:36:11.210
And I looked through the script.

1966
01:36:11.510 --> 01:36:13.790
There is no real evidence in the script.

1967
01:36:14.070 --> 01:36:18.790
However, it was scripted for them to be sitting next to each other on the panel.

1968
01:36:18.950 --> 01:36:20.150
But they're not.

1969
01:36:20.610 --> 01:36:21.770
Same for the wedding.

1970
01:36:21.770 --> 01:36:26.230
They're not at the same table at the wedding, and there are no scenes with the two of them

1971
01:36:26.230 --> 01:36:27.270
in deleted scenes.

1972
01:36:27.610 --> 01:36:28.510
That's correct.

1973
01:36:28.950 --> 01:36:35.870
I think it was decided on the day with Ken Kwapis, and I vaguely remember this as they

1974
01:36:35.870 --> 01:36:38.070
were telling us where to sit on the panel.

1975
01:36:39.070 --> 01:36:42.510
Erin's happy ending is finding her parents, not finding a man.

1976
01:36:43.110 --> 01:36:47.190
And so Ken sat them apart from one another.

1977
01:36:47.190 --> 01:36:51.110
So that the focus could be on her reuniting with her mom and dad.

1978
01:36:51.270 --> 01:36:51.590
That's right.

1979
01:36:51.870 --> 01:36:55.810
And there are scenes of the three of them talking in deleted scenes.

1980
01:36:55.970 --> 01:36:57.970
I'm going to bring it up for finale part two.

1981
01:36:58.670 --> 01:37:02.770
This part ends with the camera panning the audience.

1982
01:37:03.350 --> 01:37:05.230
Creed is in the way back.

1983
01:37:05.370 --> 01:37:06.650
He's got a full beard.

1984
01:37:07.110 --> 01:37:09.950
He is watching all of it and tearing up.

1985
01:37:09.950 --> 01:37:16.010
Well, Angela, like I said, I was really happy for you because you were off this day.

1986
01:37:16.010 --> 01:37:19.390
You were getting to pursue this opportunity that was so special.

1987
01:37:19.770 --> 01:37:26.330
But at the end of this scene, when we finished our last shot, I'm getting choked up thinking

1988
01:37:26.330 --> 01:37:26.950
about it.

1989
01:37:27.070 --> 01:37:28.450
And I wish you could have been there.

1990
01:37:28.610 --> 01:37:36.670
But the whole auditorium of people stood up and gave us a standing ovation for like three

1991
01:37:36.670 --> 01:37:37.030
minutes.

1992
01:37:37.730 --> 01:37:40.110
It was really moving.

1993
01:37:40.390 --> 01:37:42.110
Like the cameras were off.

1994
01:37:42.410 --> 01:37:42.530
Yeah.

1995
01:37:42.710 --> 01:37:43.950
The acting was over.

1996
01:37:44.350 --> 01:37:46.270
And it took us by surprise.

1997
01:37:46.590 --> 01:37:48.110
We have pictures of it.

1998
01:37:48.270 --> 01:37:50.690
